  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The mode of a random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ is the value of $x$ at the global maximum of $f(x).$\n\nTo find the global maximum of $f(x),$ we find the critical points of $f(x)$ and then apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, we find the critical points.\n\n- The piece of $f(x)$ defined on $x\\ge 0$ is $f(x)=4{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =8x{e}^{-2x}-8{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}=8{e}^{-2x}x(1-x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n8{e}^{-2x}x(1-x)\\,⟹\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nAll of these critical points are within the interval $[0,\\infty ),$ so we keep them.\n\n- We've already considered the endpoint $x=0.$\n\nNow, we evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points $x=0,1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =4\\cdot {0}^{2}{e}^{-0}=0 \\\\ f(1) & =4\\cdot {1}^{2}{e}^{-2}=4{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compare all of the values above. The largest value is\n\n$$\nf(1)=4{e}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mode of $X$ is $1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
